.TH STACK 1 "2026-08-12" "ragfarm" "ragfarm service lifecycle"
.SH NAME
stack.sh \- start, stop and health-check the whole ragfarm stack; the single source of truth for what "the whole stack" is
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B scripts/stack.sh
.RI { start | stop | restart | status | health | list }
.SH DESCRIPTION
Thirteen services across two runtimes \- systemd on the host, docker compose in
containers \- with a start order that matters and two failure modes that return
HTTP 200 while being broken.
.B stack.sh
is the one command that knows all of it.
.PP
It is also the
.B inventory.
The
.B SERVICES
array near the top of the script, plus the vLLM slots read live from the
registry, is the authoritative list of what this deployment consists of. Adding
a service means adding one row: the table, the health check, the start order and
.B list
all follow from it. Nothing else needs editing, and nothing else should carry a
second copy of the list.
.SH SUBCOMMANDS
.TP
.B status
The full picture: the service table, then systemd unit states with their boot
policy. Read-only, safe at any time. This is the one to run first.
.TP
.B health
The same probes without the systemd section. Exits non-zero if anything required
is not OK, so it works in a script or a cron check.
.TP
.B list
Print the inventory \- name, kind, unit or container, probe URL \- and exit.
Probes nothing. Use it to see what
.B is
covered before trusting a green run.
.TP
.B start
Host services first, then containers, then wait and report health. vLLM slots
are started
.B one at a time,
each waited for, because two instances profiling GPU memory concurrently kill
each other \- see
.BR activate_llm (1)
\(-> WHY SLOTS START SEQUENTIALLY. Note that
.B "systemctl start a b c"
does
.B not
serialise, which is why this loops instead.
.TP
.B stop
Containers first, then host services in reverse start order.
.TP
.B restart
.B stop
then
.BR start .
.SH THE STATE COLUMN
.TP
.B [OK]
Probe returned an expected status, and any depth check passed.
.TP
.BR [NOT_OK: " \fIcode\fR" ]
Followed by why, in operator terms: whether anything is listening at all, and
whether the unit or container behind it is running. Code
.B 000
means no listener \- nothing answered the socket.
.TP
.B [DEGRADED]
The service answered correctly and is
.B still broken.
See
.B DEPTH CHECKS
below. This is the state worth caring about.
.TP
.B [OFF]
Defined in the architecture but deliberately not deployed. Does not count
against health.
.B mcp-fs
is the only one today.
.TP
.B [ABSENT]
The systemd unit is not installed on this machine. Counts as a failure, because
something in the inventory has no implementation here.
.SH DEPTH CHECKS
Two services can return a perfectly good 200 while being useless. Both were live
on 2026-08-12, and the previous six-endpoint health check reported the stack as
fully healthy through them.
.TP
.B mcpo
.RI "Route " /rag/openapi.json " answers 200 with " paths: []
\- the gateway is up and
.B zero tools are mounted behind it.
The model then has no
.BR search_corpus ,
and every answer that should have been grounded quietly stops being grounded.
Nothing logs an error. The check counts the mounted paths on
.I /rag
and
.IR /placement ;
the fix is
.IR scripts/mcpo-heal.sh ,
which waits for the MCP backends to accept connections and restarts mcpo once.
.TP
.B drawio-viewer
nginx serves the landing page happily while
.I js/viewer-static.min.js
404s, because the 153 MB webapp mirror is gitignored and a fresh clone has only
the two tracked HTML files. Every in-chat diagram then renders as an empty white
box with no error anywhere. The check fetches the viewer JS itself; the fix is
.IR scripts/fetch-drawio-viewer.sh .
.PP
The general rule this encodes:
.B if a 200 can lie, probe the thing the 200 is supposed to promise.
.SH WHY SOME SERVICES ARE HEALTHY AT 406
The MCP backends \- rag-retrieval, mcp-placement, mcp-host-control \- speak
streamable-http. A plain
.B GET
on
.I /mcp
is answered
.B "406 Not Acceptable"
by the application itself, which is proof it is alive and speaking the protocol.
Insisting on 200 would report every MCP backend as permanently down. Each row
therefore carries an
.B allowlist
of acceptable codes rather than assuming 200.
.SH SLOTS ARE NOT HARD-CODED
Which vLLM slots exist comes from
.B active[]
in
.I models/llm/active.json
at run time, not from a list in this script. Bind a slot with
.BR activate_llm (1)
and it appears here automatically, with the model name in
.BR list ;
clear one and it stops being reported as missing. The two files cannot drift
because there is only one list.
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS After a reboot, before anything else
.RS
.nf
scripts/stack.sh status
.fi
.RE
.PP
This is the check that would have saved an hour on 2026-08-12: the box came up
with the containers running and both vLLM slots down, and Open WebUI reported
only "No models available".
.SS Is the stack actually serving, for a script
.RS
.nf
scripts/stack.sh health || echo "stack is degraded"
.fi
.RE
.SS What is even being checked?
.RS
.nf
scripts/stack.sh list
.fi
.RE
.SS Full bring-up on a cold machine
.RS
.nf
scripts/stack.sh start
.fi
.RE
.PP
Slots come up one at a time and each is waited for, so this is slow by design on
a cold FlashInfer JIT \- minutes, not seconds, the first time a checkpoint is
served.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Everything required is OK.
.B [OFF]
services do not count against this.
.TP
.B 1
At least one service is NOT_OK, DEGRADED or ABSENT.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I scripts/stack.sh
The inventory lives in the
.B SERVICES
array at the top. One row per service.
.TP
.I models/llm/active.json
Supplies the vLLM slots. See
.BR active.json (1).
.TP
.I infra/compose.yaml
The container half of the stack.
.TP
.I scripts/mcpo-heal.sh
Fixes the mcpo boot race that the depth check detects.
.TP
.I scripts/fetch-drawio-viewer.sh
Rehydrates the draw.io mirror that the depth check detects as missing.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR activate_llm (1),
.BR fetch_llm (1),
.BR active.json (1),
.BR env (1),
.BR setup_openwebui (1)
.PP
.I docs/deployment.md
for the running system;
.I docs/decisions/ADR-0013
for the engine split.
.SH NOTES
Run as
.BR dave ,
never as root.
.B sudo
is used only for the specific
.B systemctl
operations the lifecycle commands need.
